/F4 11 Tf 0 -26.84 Td(The simplest form of genetic algorithm involves three types of operators: selection, crossover \(single point\),)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(and mutation.)Tj
/F7 11 Tf 0 -26.4 Td(Selection)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( This operator selects chromosomes in the population for reproduction. The fitter the chromosome,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the more times it is likely to be selected to reproduce.)Tj
/F7 11 Tf 0 -26.4 Td(Crossover)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( This operator randomly chooses a locus and exchanges the subsequences before and after that locus)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(between two chromosomes to create two offspring. For example, the strings 10000100 and 11111111 could be)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(crossed over after the third locus in each to produce the two offspring 10011111 and 11100100. The crossover)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(operator roughly mimics biological recombination between two single-chromosome \(haploid\) organisms.)Tj
/F7 11 Tf 0 -26.4 Td(Mutation)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( This operator randomly flips some of the bits in a chromosome. For example, the string 00000100)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(might be mutated in its second position to yield 01000100. Mutation can occur at each bit position in a string)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(with some probability, usually very small \(e.g., 0.001\).)Tj
