/F4 11 Tf 0 -27.368 Td(In the preceding sections I used the word "search" to describe what GAs do. It is important at this point to)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(contrast this meaning of "search" with its other meanings in computer science.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(There are at least three \(overlapping\) meanings of "search":)Tj
/F7 11 Tf 0 -26.4 Td(Search for stored data)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( Here the problem is to efficiently retrieve information stored in computer memory.)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Suppose you have a large database of names and addresses stored in some ordered way. What is the best way)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(to search for the record corresponding to a given last name? "Binary search" is one method for efficiently)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(finding the desired record. Knuth \(1973\) describes and analyzes many such search methods.)Tj
/F7 11 Tf 0 -26.4 Td(Search for paths to goals)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( Here the problem is to efficiently find a set of actions that will move from a given)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(initial state to a given goal. This form)Tj
( of search is central to many approaches in artificial intelligence. A)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(simple example\227all too familiar to anyone who has taken a course in AI\227is the "8-puzzle," illustrated in)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(figure 1.2. A set of tiles numbered 1\2268 are placed in a square, leaving one space empty. Sliding one of the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(adjacent tiles into the blank space is termed a "move." Figure 1.2a illustrates the problem of finding a set of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(moves from the initial state to the state in which all the tiles are in order. A partial search tree corresponding)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(to this problem is illustrated in figure 1.2b The "root" node represents the initial state, the nodes branching out)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(from it represent all possible results of one move from that state, and so on down the tree. The search)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(algorithms discussed in most AI contexts are methods for efficiently finding the best \(here, the shortest\) path)Tj
/F8 11 Tf 135.601 692.54 Td(Chapter 1: Genetic Algorithms: An Overview)Tj
