0 -26.4 Td(The Schema Theorem as stated in equation 1.2 is a lower bound, since it deals only with the destructive)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(effects of crossover and mutation. However, crossover is believed to be a major source of the GA's power,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(with the ability to recombine instances of good schemas to form instances of equally good or better)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(higher-order schemas. The supposition that this is the process by which GAs work is known as the Building)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Block Hypothesis \(Goldberg 1989a\). \(For work on quantifying this "constructive" power of crossover, see)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Holland 1975, Thierens and Goldberg 1993, and Spears 1993.\))Tj
0 -26.4 Td(In evaluating a population of)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( n)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( strings, the GA is implicitly estimating the average fitnesses of all schemas)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(that are present in the population, and increasing or decreasing their representation according to the Schema)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Theorem. This simultaneous implicit evaluation of large numbers of schemas in a population of)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( n)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( strings is)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(known as)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( implicit paralelism)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( \(Holland 1975\). The effect of selection is to gradually bias the sampling)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(procedure toward instances of schemas whose fitness is estimated to be above average. Over time, the)Tj
