0 -13.2 Td(accuracy will be discussed in chapter 4.\))Tj
0 -26.4 Td(The Schema Theorem and the Building Block Hypothesis deal primarily with the roles of selection and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(crossover in GAs. What is the role of mutation? Holland \(1975\) proposed that mutation is what prevents the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(loss of diversity at a given bit position. For example, without mutation, every string in the population might)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(come to have a one at the first bit position, and there would then be no way to obtain a string beginning with a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(zero. Mutation provides an "insurance policy" against such fixation.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(The Schema Theorem given in equation 1.1 applies not only to schemas but to any subset of strings in the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(search space. The reason for specifically focusing on schemas is that they \(in particular, short,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(high-average-fitness schemas\) are a good description of the types of building blocks that are combined)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(effectively by single-point crossover. A belief underlying this formulation of the GA is that schemas will be a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(good description of the relevant building blocks of a good solution. GA researchers have defined other types)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(of crossover operators that deal with different types of building blocks, and have analyzed the generalized)Tj
0 -13.2 Td("schemas" that a given crossover operator effectively manipulates \(Radcliffe 1991; Vose 1991\).)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(The Schema Theorem and some of its purported implications for the)Tj
( behavior of GAs have recently been the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(subject of much critical discussion in the GA community. These criticisms and the new approaches to GA)Tj
