/F9 19 Tf 0 678.992 Td 0.000 Tc(Chapter 2: Genetic Algorithms in Problem Solving)Tj
/F9 15.8 Tf 0 -32.842 Td(Overview)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -27.368 Td(Like other computational systems inspired by natural systems, genetic algorithms have been used in two)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(ways: as techniques for solving technological problems, and as simplified scientific models that can answer)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(questions about nature. This chapter gives several case studies of GAs as problem solvers; chapter 3 gives)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(several case studies of GAs used as scientific models. Despite this seemingly clean split between engineering)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(and scientific applications, it is often not clear on which side of the fence a particular project sits. For)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(example, the work by Hillis described in chapter 1 above and the two other automatic-programming projects)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(described below have produced results that, apart from their potential technological applications, may be of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(interest in evolutionary biology. Likewise, several of the "artificial life" projects described in chapter 3 have)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(potential problem-solving applications. In short, the "clean split" between GAs for engineering and GAs for)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(science is actually fuzzy, but this fuzziness\227and its potential for useful feedback between problem-solving)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(and scientific-modeling applications\227is part of what makes GAs and other adaptive-computation methods)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(particularly interesting.)Tj
/F9 15.8 Tf 0 -57.64 Td(2.1 EVOLVING COMPUTER PROGRAMS)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -40.568 Td(Automatic programming\227i.e., having computer programs automatically write computer programs\227has a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(long history in the field of artificial intelligence. Many different approaches have been tried, but as yet no)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(general method has been found for automatically producing the complex and robust programs needed for real)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(applications.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Some early evolutionary computation techniques were aimed at automatic programming. The evolutionary)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(programming approach of Fogel, Owens, and Walsh \(1966\) evolved simple programs in the form of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(finite-state machines. Early applications of genetic algorithms to simple automatic-programming tasks were)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(performed by Cramer \(1985\) and by Fujiki and Dickinson \(1987\), among others. The recent resurgence of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(interest in automatic programming with genetic algorithms has been, in part,)Tj
( spurred by John Koza's work on)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(evolving Lisp programs via "genetic programming.")Tj
0 -26.4 Td(The idea of evolving computer programs rather than writing them is very appealing to many. This is)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(particularly true in the case of programs for massively parallel computers, as the difficulty of programming)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(such computers is a major obstacle to their widespread use. Hillis's work on evolving efficient sorting)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(networks is one example of automatic programming for parallel computers. My own work with Crutchfield,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Das, and Hraber on evolving cellular automata to perform computations is an example of automatic)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(programming for a very different type of parallel architecture.)Tj
/F9 13.2 Tf 0 -28.6 Td(Evolving Lisp Programs)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -26.84 Td(John Koza \(1992,1994\) has used a form of the genetic algorithm to evolve Lisp programs to perform various)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(tasks. Koza claims that his method\227 "genetic programming" \(GP\)\227has the potential to produce programs of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the necessary complexity and robustness for general automatic programming. Programs in Lisp can easily be)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(expressed in the form of a "parse tree," the object the GA will work on.)Tj
/F8 11 Tf 237.384 -55.934 Td(27)Tj
