0 -13.2 Td(capabilities of GP. Does it work well because the space of Lisp expressions is in some sense "dense" with)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(correct programs for the relatively simple tasks Koza and other GP researchers have tried? This was given as)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(one reason for the success of the artificial intelligence program AM \(Lenat and Brown 1984\), which evolved)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Lisp expressions to discover "interesting" conjectures in mathematics, such as the Goldbach conjecture \(every)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(even number is the sum of two primes\). Koza refuted)Tj
( this hypothesis about GP by demonstrating how difficult)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(it is to randomly generate a successful program to perform some of the tasks for which GP evolves successful)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(programs. However, one could speculate that the space of Lisp expressions \(with a given set of functions and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(terminals\) is dense with useful intermediate-size building blocks for the tasks on which GP has been)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(successful. GP's ability to find solutions quickly \(e.g., within 10 generations using a population of 300\) lends)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(credence to this speculation.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(GP also has not been compared systematically with other techniques that could search in the space of parse)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(trees. For example, it would be interesting to know if a hill climbing technique could do as well as GP on the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(examples Koza gives. One test of this was reported by O'Reilly and Oppacher \(1994a,b\), who defined a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(mutation operator for parse trees and used it to compare GP with a simple hill-climbing technique similar to)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(random-mutation hill climbing \(see computer exercise 4 of chapter 1\) and with simulated annealing \(a more)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(sophisticated hill-climbing technique\). Comparisons were made on five problems, including the block)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(stacking problem described above. On each of the five, simulated annealing either equaled or significantly)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(outperformed GP in terms of the number of runs on which a correct solution was found and the average)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(number of fitness-function evaluations needed to find a correct program. On two out of the five, the simple)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(hill climber either equaled or exceeded the performance of GP.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Though five problems is not many for such a comparison in view of the number of problems on which GP has)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(been tried, these results bring into question the claim \(Koza 1992\) that the crossover operator is a major)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(contributor to GP's success. O'Reilly and Oppacher \(1994a\) speculate from their results that the parse-tree)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(representation "may be a more fundamental asset to program induction than any particular search technique,")Tj
0 -13.2 Td(and that "perhaps the concept of building blocks is irrelevant to GP." These speculations are well worth)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(further investigation, and it is imperative to characterize the types of problems for which crossover is a useful)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(operator and for which a GA will be likely to outperform gradient-ascent strategies such as hill climbing and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(simulated annealing. Some work toward those goals will be described in chapter 4.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Some other questions about GP:)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Will the technique scale up to more complex problems for which larger programs are needed?)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Will the technique work if the function and terminal sets are large?)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(How well do the evolved programs generalize to cases not in the set of fitness cases? In most of Koza's)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(examples, the cases used to compute fitness are samples from a much larger set of possible fitness cases. GP)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(very often finds a program that is correct on all the given fitness cases, but not enough has been reported on)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(how well these programs do on the "out-of-sample" cases. We need to know the extent to which GP)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(produces)Tj
( programs that generalize well after seeing only a small fraction of the possible fitness cases.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(To what extent can programs be optimized for correctness, size, and efficiency at the same time?)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Genetic programming's success on a wide range of problems should encourage future research addressing)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(these questions. \(For examples of more recent work on GP, see Kinnear 1994.\))Tj
