/F4 11 Tf 0 -26.84 Td(A quite different example of automatic programming by genetic algorithms is found in work done by James)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Crutchfield, Rajarshi Das, Peter Hraber, and myself on evolving cellular automata to perform computations)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(\(Mitchell, Hraber, and Crutchfield 1993; Mitchell, Crutchfield, and Hraber 1994a; Crutchfield and Mitchell)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(1994; Das, Mitchell, and Crutchfield 1994\). This project has elements of both problem solving and scientific)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(modeling. One motivation is to understand how natural evolution creates systems in which "emergent)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(computation" takes place\227that is, in which the actions of simple components with limited information and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(communication give rise to coordinated global information processing. Insect colonies, economic systems, the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(immune system, and the brain have all been cited as examples of systems in which such emergent)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(computation occurs \(Forrest 1990; Langton 1992\). However, it is not well understood)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( how)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( these natural)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(systems perform computations. Another motivation is to find ways to engineer sophisticated emergent)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(computation in decentralized multi-processor systems, using ideas from how natural decentralized systems)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(compute. Such systems have many of the desirable properties for computer systems mentioned in chapter 1:)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(they are sophisticated, robust, fast, and adaptable information processors. Using ideas from such systems to)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(design new types of parallel computers might yield great progress in computer science.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(One of the simplest systems in which emergent computation can be studied is a one-dimensional binary-state)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(cellular automaton \(CA\)\227a one-dimensional lattice of)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( N)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( two-state machines \("cells"\), each of which)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(changes its state as a function only of the current states in a local neighborhood. \(The well-known "game of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Life" \(Berlekamp, Conway, and Guy 1982\) is an example of a two-dimensional CA.\) A one-dimensional CA)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(is illustrated in figure 2.5. The lattice starts out with an initial configuration of cell states \(zeros and ones\) and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(this configuration changes in discrete time steps in which all cells are updated simultaneously according to the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(CA "rule")Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( \306)Tj
/F4 11 Tf (. \(Here I use the term "state" to refer to refer to a local state)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( s)Tj
/F6 7.6 Tf 324.643 -1.681 Td(i)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 2.124 1.681 Td(\227the value of the single cell at site)Tj
/F6 11 Tf -326.767 -13.2 Td(i)Tj
/F4 11 Tf (. The term "configuration" will refer to the pattern of local states over the entire lattice.\))Tj
0 -208.309 Td(Figure 2.5: Illustration of a one-dimensional, binary-state, nearest-neighbor \(r = 1\) cellular automaton with)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(N = 11. Both the lattice and the rule table for updating the lattice are illustrated. The lattice configuration is)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(shown over one time step. The cellular automaton has periodic boundary conditions: the lattice is viewed as a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(circle, with the leftmost cell the right neighbor of the rightmost cell, and vice versa.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(A CA rule)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( \306)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( can be expressed as a lookup table \("rule table"\) that lists,)Tj
0 -39.6 Td(for each local neighborhood, the update state for the neighborhood's central cell. For a binary-state CA, the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(update states are referred to as the "output bits" of the rule table. In a one-dimensional CA, a neighborhood)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(consists of a cell and its)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( r)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( \("radius"\) neighbors on either side. The CA illustrated in figure 2.5 has)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( r)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( = 1. It)Tj
