0 -26.4 Td(Cellular automata have been studied extensively as mathematical objects, as models of natural systems, and as)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(architectures for fast, reliable parallel computation. \(For overviews of CA theory and applications, see Toffoli)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(and Margolus 1987 and Wolfram 1986.\) However, the difficulty of understanding the emergent behavior of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(CAs or of designing CAs to have desired behavior has up to now severely limited their use in science and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(engineering and for general computation. Our goal is to use GAs as a method for engineering CAs to perform)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(computations.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Typically, a CA performing a computation means that the input to the computation is encoded as an initial)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(configuration, the output is read off the configuration after some time step, and the intermediate steps that)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(transform the input to the output are taken as the steps in the computation. The "program" emerges from the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(CA rule being obeyed by each cell. \(Note that this use of CAs as computers differs from the impractical)Tj
0 -39.6 Td(though theoretically interesting method of constructing a universal Turing machine in a CA; see Mitchell,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Crutchfield, and Hraber 1994b for a comparison of these two approaches.\))Tj
0 -26.4 Td(The behavior of one-dimensional CAs is often illustrated by a "space-time diagram"\227a plot of lattice)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(configurations over a range of time steps, with ones given as black cells and zeros given as white cells and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(with time increasing down the page. Figure 2.6 shows such a diagram for a binary-state)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( r)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( = 3 CA in which the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(rule table's output bits were filled in at random. It is shown iterating on a randomly generated initial)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(configuration. Random-looking patterns, such as the one shown, are typical for the vast majority of CAs. To)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(produce CAs that can perform sophisticated parallel computations, the genetic algorithm must evolve CAs in)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(which the actions of the cells are not random-looking but are coordinated with one another so as to produce)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the desired result. This coordination must, of course, happen in the absence of any central processor or)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(memory directing the coordination.)Tj
0 -212.606 Td(Figure 2.6: Space-time diagram for a randomly generated r = 3 cellular automaton, iterating on a randomly)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(generated initial configuration. N = 149 sites are shown, with time increasing down the page. Here cells with)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(state 0 are white and cells with state 1 are black. \(This and the other space-time diagrams given here were)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(generated using the program "la1d" written by James P. Crutchfield.\))Tj
/F8 11 Tf 122.456 668.006 Td(Chapter 2: Genetic Algorithms in Problem Solving)Tj
