/F4 11 Tf 0 687 Td 0.000 Tc(Why does \306d, for example, perform relatively well on the )Tj
281.767 0 Td( task? In figure 2.8d it can be seen that,)Tj
-281.767 -13.2 Td(although the patterns eventually converge to fixed points, there is a transient phase during which spatial and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(temporal transfer of information about the density in local regions takes place. This local information interacts)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(with other local information to produce the desired final state. Roughly, \306)Tj
/F4 7.6 Tf 328.438 -1.681 Td(d)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 3.819 1.681 Td( successively classifies "local")Tj
-332.257 -13.2 Td(densities with a locality range that increases with time. In regions where there is some ambiguity, a "signal" is)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(propagated. This is seen either as a checkerboard pattern propagated in both spatial directions or as a vertical)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(black-to-white boundary. These signals indicate that the classification is to be made at a larger scale. \(Such)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(signals for resolving ambiguities are precisely what was lacking in the majority rule on this task.\) Note that)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(regions centered about each signal locally have )Tj
231.959 0 Td( The consequence is that the signal patterns can)Tj
-231.959 -13.2 Td(propagate, since the density of patterns with )Tj
218.231 0 Td( is neither increased nor decreased under the rule. The)Tj
-218.231 -13.2 Td(creation and interactions of these signals can be interpreted as the locus of the computation being performed)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(by the CA\227they form its emergent program.)Tj
0 -39.6 Td(The above explanation of how \306)Tj
/F4 7.6 Tf 146.333 -1.681 Td(d)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 3.819 1.681 Td( performs the )Tj
82.887 0 Td( task is an informal one obtained by careful scrutiny of)Tj
-233.04 -13.2 Td(many space-time patterns. Can we understand more rigorously how the rules evolved by the GA perform the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(desired computation? Understanding the results of GA evolution is a general problem\227typically the GA is)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(asked to find individuals that achieve high fitness but is not told how that high fitness is to be attained. One)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(could say that this is analogous to the difficulty biologists have in understanding the products of natural)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(evolution \(e.g., us\). We computational evolutionists have similar problems, since we do not specify what)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(solution evolution is supposed to create; we ask only that it find some solution. In many cases, particularly in)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(automatic-programming applications, it is difficult to understand exactly how an evolved high-fitness)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(individual works. In genetic programming, for example, the evolved programs are often very long and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(complicated, with many irrelevant components attached to the core program performing the desired)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(computation. It is usually a lot of work\227and sometimes almost impossible\227to figure out by hand what that)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(core program is. The problem is even more difficult in the case of cellular automata, since the emergent)Tj
0 -13.2 Td("program" performed by a given CA is almost always impossible to extract from the bits of the rule table.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(A more promising approach is to examine the space-time patterns created by the CA and to "reconstruct")Tj
0 -13.2 Td(from those patterns what the algorithm is. Crutchfield and Hanson have developed a general method for)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(reconstructing and understanding the "intrinsic" computation embedded in space-time patterns in terms of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td("regular domains," "particles" and "particle interactions" \(Hanson and Crutchfield, 1992; Crutchfield and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Hanson 1993\). This method is part of their "computational mechanics" framework for understanding)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(computation in physical systems. A detailed discussion of computational mechanics and particle-based)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(computation is beyond the scope of this chapter. Very briefly, for those familiar with formal language theory,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(regular domains are regions of spacetime consisting of words in the same regular language\227that is, they are)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(regions that are computationally simple. Particles are localized boundaries between regular domains. In)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(computational mechanics, particles are identified as information carriers, and collisions between particles are)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(identified as the loci of important information processing. Particles and particle interactions form a high-level)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(language for describing computation in spatially extended systems such as CAs. Figure 2.9 hints at this higher)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(level of description: to produce it we filtered the regular domains from the space-time behavior of a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(GA-evolved CA to leave only the particles and their interactions, in terms of which the emergent algorithm of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the CA can be understood.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(The application of computational mechanics to the understanding of rules evolved by the GA is discussed)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(further in Crutchfield and Mitchell 1994, in Das, Mitchell, and Crutchfield 1994, and in Das, Crutchfield,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Mitchell, and Hanson 1995. In the last two papers, we used particles and)Tj
/F8 11 Tf 122.456 640.2 Td(Chapter 2: Genetic Algorithms in Problem Solving)Tj
