/F9 13.2 Tf 0 684.8 Td 0.000 Tc(Predicting Protein Structure)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -26.84 Td(One of the most promising and rapidly growing areas of GA application is data analysis and prediction in)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(molecular biology. GAs have been used for, among other things, interpreting nuclear magnetic resonance data)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(to determine the structure of DNA \(Lucasius and Kateman 1989\), finding)Tj
( the correct ordering for an)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(unordered group of DNA fragments \(Parsons, Forrest, and Burks, in press\), and predicting protein structure.)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Here I will describe one particular project in which a GA was used to predict the structure of a protein.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Proteins are the fundamental functional building blocks of all biological cells. The main purpose of DNA in a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(cell is to encode instructions for building up proteins out of amino acids; the proteins in turn carry out most of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the structural and metabolic functions of the cell. A protein is made up of a sequence of amino acids)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(connected by peptide bonds. The length of the sequence varies from protein to protein but is typically on the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(order of 100 amino acids. Owing to electrostatic and other physical forces, the sequence "folds up" to a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(particular three-dimensional structure. It is this three-dimensional structure that primarily determines the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(protein's function. The three-dimensional structure of a Crambin protein \(a plant-seed protein consisting of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(46 amino acids\) is illustrated in figure 2.14. The three-dimensional structure of a protein is determined by the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(particular sequence of its amino acids, but it is not currently known precisely how a given sequence leads to a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(given structure. In fact, being able to predict a protein's structure from its amino acid sequence is one of the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(most important unsolved problems of molecular biology and biophysics. Not only would a successful)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(prediction algorithm be a tremendous advance in the understanding of the biochemical mechanisms of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(proteins, but, since such an algorithm could conceivably be used to)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( design)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( proteins to carry out specific)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(functions, it would have profound, far-reaching effects on biotechnology and the treatment of disease.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Recently there has been considerable effort toward developing methods such as GAs and neural networks for)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(automatically predicting protein structures \(see, for example, Hunter, Searls, and Shavlik 1993\). The relatively)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(simple GA prediction project of Steffen Schulze-Kremer \(1992\) illustrates one way in which GAs can be)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(used on this task; it also illustrates some potential pitfalls.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Schulze-Kremer took the amino acid sequence of the Crambin protein and used a GA to search in the space)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(of possible structures for one that would fit well with Crambin's amino acid sequence. The most)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(straight-forward way to describe the structure of a protein is to list the three-dimensional coordinates of each)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(amino acid, or even each atom. In principle, a GA could use such a representation, evolving vectors of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(coordinates to find one that resulted in a plausible structure. But, because of a number of difficulties with that)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(representation \(e.g., the usual crossover and mutation operators would be too likely to create physically)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(impossible structures\), Schulze-Kremer instead described protein structures using "torsion angles"\227roughly,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the angles made by the peptide bonds connecting amino acids and the angles made by bonds in an amino)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(acid's "side chain." \(See Dickerson and Geis 1969 for an overview of how three-dimensional protein structure)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(is measured.\) Schulze-Kremer used 10 tor)Tj
(sion angles to describe each of the)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( N)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( \(46 in the case of Crambin\))Tj
0 -13.2 Td(amino acids in the sequence for a given protein. This collection of)Tj
/F6 11 Tf (N)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( sets of 10 torsion angles completely)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(defines the three-dimensional structure of the protein. A chromosome, representing a candidate structure with)Tj
/F6 11 Tf 0 -13.2 Td(N)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( amino acids, thus contains)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( N)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( sets of ten real numbers. This representation is illustrated in figure 2.15.)Tj
