-417.411 -26.4 Td(It is known that single-layer networks can learn only those classes of input-output mappings that are "linearly)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(separable" \(Rumelhart et al. 1986\). As an "environment" for the evolving learning rules, Chalmers used 30)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(different linearly separable mappings to be learned via the learning rules. The mappings always had a single)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(output unit and between two and seven input units.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(The fitness of each chromosome \(learning rule\) was determined as follows. A subset of 20 mappings was)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(selected from the full set of 30 mappings. For each mapping, 12 training examples were selected. For each of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(these mappings, a network was created with the appropriate number of input units for the given mapping)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(\(each network had one output unit\). The network's weights were initialized randomly. The network was run)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(on the training set for some number of epochs \(typically 10\), using the learning rule specified by the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(chromosome. The performance of the learning rule on a given mapping was a function of the network's error)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(on the training set, with low error meaning high performance. The overall fitness of the learning rule was a)Tj
/F8 11 Tf 122.456 684.872 Td(Chapter 2: Genetic Algorithms in Problem Solving)Tj
