/F4 11 Tf 0 687 Td 0.000 Tc(function of the average error of the 20 networks over the chosen subset of 20 mappings\227low average error)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(translated to high fitness. This fitness was then transformed to be a percentage, where a high percentage meant)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(high fitness.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Using this fitness measure, the GA was run on a population of 40 learning rules, with two-point crossover)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(and standard mutation. The crossover rate was 0.8 and the mutation rate was 0.01. Typically, over 1000)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(generations, the fitness of the best learning rules in the population rose from between 40% and 60% in the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(initial generation \(indicating no significant)Tj
( learning ability\) to between 80% and 98%, with a mean \(over)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(several runs\) of about 92%. The fitness of the delta rule is around 98%, and on one out of a total of ten runs)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the GA discovered this rule. On three of the ten runs, the GA discovered slight variations of this rule with)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(lower fitness.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(These results show that, given a somewhat constrained representation, the GA was able to evolve a successful)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(learning rule for simple single-layer networks. The extent to which this method can find learning rules for)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(more complex networks \(including networks with hidden units\) remains an open question, but these results)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(are a first step in that direction. Chalmers suggested that it is unlikely that evolutionary methods will discover)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(learning methods that are more powerful than back-propagation, but he speculated that the GA might be a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(powerful method for discovering learning rules for unsupervised learning paradigms \(e.g., reinforcement)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(learning\) or for new classes of network architectures \(e.g., recurrent networks\).)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Chalmers also performed a study of the generality of the evolved learning rules. He tested each of the best)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(evolved rules on the ten mappings that had not been used in the fitness calculation for that rule \(the "test set"\).)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(The mean fitness of the best rules on the original mappings was 92%, and Chalmers found that the mean)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(fitness of these rules on the test set was 91.9%. In short, the evolved rules were quite general.)Tj
