/F4 11 Tf 0 -27.368 Td(Genetic algorithms have been for the most part techniques applied by computer scientists and engineers to)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(solve practical problems. However, John Holland's original work on the subject was meant not only to)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(develop adaptive computer systems for problem solving but also to shed light, via computer models, on the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(mechanisms of natural evolution.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(The idea of using computer models to study evolution is still relatively new and is not widely accepted in the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(evolutionary biology community. Traditionally, biologists have used several approaches to understanding)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(evolution, including the following:)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Examining the fossil record to determine how evolution has proceeded over geological time.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Examining existing biological systems in their natural habitats in order to understand the evolutionary forces)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(at work in the process of adaptation. This includes both understanding the role of genetic mechanisms \(such as)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(geographical effects on mating\) and understanding the function of various physical and behavioral)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(characteristics of organisms so as to infer the selective forces responsible for the evolution of these)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(adaptations.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Performing laboratory experiments in which evolution over many generations in a population of relatively)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(simple organisms is studied and controlled. Many such experiments involve fruit flies)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( \(Drosophila\))Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( because)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(their life span and their reproductive cycle are short enough that experimenters can observe natural selection)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(over many generations in a reasonable amount of time.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Studying evolution at the molecular level by looking at how DNA and RNA change over time under particular)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(genetic mechanisms, or by determining how different evolutionarily related species compare at the level of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(DNA so as to reconstruct "phylogenies" \(evolutionary family histories of related species\).)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Developing mathematical models of evolution in the form of equations \(representing properties of genotypes)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(and phenotypes and their evolution\) that can be solved \(analytically or numerically\) or approximated.)Tj
0 -39.6 Td(These are the types of methods that have produced the bulk of our current understanding of natural evolution.)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(However, such methods have a number of inherent limitations. The observed fossil record is almost certainly)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(incomplete, and what is there is often hard to interpret; in many cases what is surmised from fossils is)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(intelligent guesswork. It is hard, if not impossible, to do controlled experiments on biological systems in)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(nature, and evolutionary time scales are most often far too long for scientists to directly observe how)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(biological systems change. Evolution in systems such as)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( Drosophila)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( can be observed to a limited extent, but)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(many of the important questions in evolution \(How does speciation take place? How did multicellular)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(organisms come into being? Why did sex evolve?\) cannot be answered by merely studying evolution in)Tj
/F6 11 Tf 0 -13.2 Td(Drosophila)Tj
/F4 11 Tf (. The molecular level is often ambiguous\227for example, it is not clear what it is that individual)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(pieces of DNA encode, or how they work together to produce phenotypic traits, or even which pieces do the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(encoding and which are "junk DNA" \(noncoding regions of the chromosome\). Finally, to be solvable,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(mathematical models of evolution must be simplified greatly, and it is not obvious that the simple models)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(provide insight into real evolution.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(The invention of computers has permitted a new approach to studying evolution and other natural systems:)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(simulation. A computer program can simulate the evolution of populations of organisms over millions of)Tj
