/F4 11 Tf 0 687 Td 0.000 Tc(simulated generations, and such simulations can potentially be used to test theories about the biggest open)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(questions in evolution. Simulation experiments can do what traditional methods typically cannot: experiments)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(can be controlled, they can be repeated to see how the modification of certain parameters changes the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(behavior of the simulation, and they can be run for many simulated generations. Such computer simulations)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(are said to be "microanalytic" or "agent based." They differ from the more standard use of computers in)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(evolutionary theory to solve mathematical models \(typically systems of differential equations\) that capture)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(only the global dynamics of an evolving system. Instead, they simulate each component of the evolving)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(system and its local interactions; the global dynamics emerges from these simulated local dynamics. This)Tj
0 -13.2 Td("microanalytic" strategy is the hallmark of artificial life models.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Computer simulations have many limitations as models of real-world phenomena. Most often, they must)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(drastically simplify reality in order to be computationally tractable and for the results to be understandable. As)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(with the even simpler purely mathematical models, it is not clear that the results will apply to more realistic)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(systems. On the other hand, more realistic models take a long time to simulate, and they suffer from the same)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(problem we often face in direct studies of nature: they produce huge amounts of data that are often very hard)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(to interpret.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Such questions dog every kind of scientific model, computational or otherwise, and to date most biologists)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(have not been convinced that computer simulations can teach them much. However, with the increasing)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(power \(and decreasing cost\) of computers, and given the clear limitations of simple analytically solvable)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(models of evolution, more researchers are looking seriously at what simulation can uncover. Genetic)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(algorithms are one obvious method for microanalytic simulation of evolutionary systems. Their use in this)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(arena is also growing as a result of the rising interest among computer scientists in building computational)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(models of biological processes. Here I describe several computer modeling efforts, undertaken mainly by)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(computer scientists, and aimed at answering questions such as: How can learning during a lifetime affect the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(evolution of a species? What is the evolutionary effect of sexual selection? What is the relative density of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(different species over time in a given ecosystem? How are evolution and adaptation to be measured in an)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(observed system?)Tj
