0 -26.4 Td(Hinton and Nowlan found that without learning \(i.e., with evolution alone\) the mean fitness of the population)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(never increased over time, but figure 3.3 shows that with learning the mean fitness did increase, even though)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(what was learned by individuals was not inherited by their offspring. In this way it can be said that learning)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(can guide evolution, even without the direct transmission of acquired traits. Hinton and Nowlan interpreted)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(this increase as being due to the Baldwin effect: those individuals that were able to learn the correct)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(connections quickly tended to be selected to reproduce, and crossovers among these individuals tended to)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(increase the number of correctly fixed alleles, increasing the learning efficiency of the offspring. With this)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(simple form of learning, evolution was able to discover individuals with all their connections fixed correctly.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Figure 3.4 shows the relative frequencies of the correct, incorrect, and undecided alleles in the population)Tj
