0 -13.2 Td(pressure had been increased, the Baldwin effect would have been stronger. Figure 3.5 shows these same)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(results over an extended run. \(These results come from Belew's \(1990\) replication and extension of Hinton)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(and Nowlan's original experiments.\) This plot shows that the frequency of question marks goes down to about)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(30%. Given more time it might go down further, but under this selection regime the convergence was)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(extremely slow.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(To summarize: Learning can be a way for genetically coded partial solutions to get partial credit. A common)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(claim for learning is that it allows an organism to respond to unpredictable aspects of the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(environment\227aspects that change too quickly for evolution to track genetically. Although this is clearly one)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(benefit of learning, the Baldwin effect is different: it says that learning helps organisms adapt to genetically)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(predictable but difficult aspects of the environment, and that learning indirectly helps these adaptations)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(become genetically encoded.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(The "learning" mechanism used in Hinton and Nowlan's experiments\227random guessing\227is of course)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(completely unrealistic as a model of learning. Hinton and Nowlan \(1987, p. 500\) pointed out that "a more)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(sophisticated learning procedure only strengthens the argument for the)Tj
0 -152.447 Td(Figure 3.5: Relative frequencies of correct \(solid circles\), incorrect \(open circles\), and undecided \(solid line\))Tj
0 -13.2 Td(alleles in the population plotted over 500 generations, from Belew's replication of Hinton and Nowlan's)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(experiments. \(Reprinted from Belew 1990 by permission of the publisher. \251 1990 Complex Systems.\))Tj
0 -26.4 Td(importance of the Baldwin effect." This is true insofar as a more sophisticated learning procedure would, for)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(example, further smooth the original "needle in a haystack" fitness landscape in Hinton and Nowlan's learning)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(task, presumably by allowing more individuals to learn the correct settings. However, if the learning)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(procedure were too sophisticated\227that is, if learning the necessary trait were too easy\227there would be little)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(selection pressure for evolution to move from theability to learn the trait to a genetic encoding of that trait.)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Such tradeoffs occur in evolution and can be seen even in Hinton and Nowlan's simple model. Computer)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(simulations such as theirs can help us to understand and to measure such tradeoffs. More detailed analyses of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Hinton and Nowlan's model were performed by Belew \(1990\), Harvey \(1993\), and French and Messinger)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(\(1994\).)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(A more important departure from biological reality in this model, and one reason why the Baldwin effect)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(showed up so strongly, is the lack of a "phenotype." The fitness of an individual is a direct function of the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(alleles in its chromosome, rather than of the traits and behaviors of its phenotype. Thus, there is a direct)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(correlation here between learned adaptations and genetic variation\227in fact, they are one and the same thing.)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(What if, as in real biology, there were a big distance between the genotypic and phenotypic levels, and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(learning occurred on the phenotypic level? Would the Baldwin effect show up in that case too, transferring the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(learned adaptations into genetically encoded traits? The next subsection describes a model that is a bit closer)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(to this more realistic scenario.)Tj
/F8 11 Tf 121.235 687.047 Td(Chapter 3: Genetic Algorithms in Scientific Models)Tj
