/F4 11 Tf 0 687 Td 0.000 Tc(Fisher \(1930\) proposed that this process could result in a feedback loop between females' preference for a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(certain trait and the strength and frequency of that trait in males. \(Here I use the more common example of a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(female preference for a male trait, but sexual selection has also been observed in the other direction.\) As the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(frequency of females that prefer the trait increases, it becomes increasingly sexually advantageous for males)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(to have it, which then causes the preference genes to increase further because of increased mating between)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(females with the preference and males with the trait. Fisher termed this "runaway sexual selection.")Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Sexual selection differs from the usual notion of natural selection. The latter selects traits that help organisms)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(survive, whereas the former selects traits only on the basis of what attracts potential mates. However, the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(possession of either kind of trait accomplishes the same thing: it increases the likelihood that an organism will)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(reproduce and thus pass on the genes for the trait to its offspring.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(There are many open questions about how sexual selection works, and most of them are hard to answer using)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(traditional methods in evolutionary biology. How do particular preferences for traits \(such as elaborate)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(plumage\) arise in the first place? How fast does the presence of sexual selection affect an evolving population,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(and in what ways? What is its relative power with respect to natural selection? Some scientists believe that)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(questions such as these can best be answered by computer models. Here I will describe one such model,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(developed by Robert Collins and David Jefferson, that uses genetic algorithms. Several computer simulations)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(of sexual selection have been reported in the population genetics literature \(see, e.g., Heisler and Curtsinger)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(1990 or Otto 1991\), but Collins and Jefferson's is one of the few to use a microanalytic method based on a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(genetic algorithm. \(For other GA-based models, see Miller and Todd 1993, Todd and Miller 1993, and Miller)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(1994.\) This description will give readers a feel for the kind of modeling that is being done, the kinds of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(questions that are being addressed, and the limits of these approaches.)Tj
