/F4 11 Tf 0 687 Td 0.000 Tc(Like all mathematical models in population genetics, Kirkpatrick's model makes a number of assumptions that)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(allow it to be solved analytically: each organism has only one gene of interest; the population is assumed to be)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(infinite; each female chooses her mate by examining all the males in the population; there are no evolutionary)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(forces apart from natural selection and sexual selection on one locus \(the model does not include mutation,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(genetic drift, selection on other loci, or spatial restrictions on mating\). In addition, the solution gives only the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(equilibrium dynamics of the system, not any intermediate dynamics, whereas real systems are rarely if ever at)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(an equilibrium state. Relaxing these assumptions would make the system more realistic and perhaps more)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(predictive of real systems, but would make analytic solution intractable.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Collins and Jefferson proposed using computer simulation as a way to study the behavior of a more realistic)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(version of the model. Rather than the standard approach of using a computer to iterate a system of differential)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(equations, they used a genetic algorithm in which each organism and each interaction between organisms was)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(simulated explicitly.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(The simulation was performed on a massively parallel computer \(a Connection Machine 2\). In Collins and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Jefferson's GA the organisms were the same as in Kirkpatrick's model \(that is, each individual consisted of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(two chromosomes, one with a)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( p)Tj
