/F4 11 Tf 0 687 Td 0.000 Tc(an initial population in which the allele)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( t)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( = 1 was already present in significant numbers. But how does a new)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(male trait come about in the first place? And once it is discovered in one organism, how does it invade a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(population? Collins and Jefferson tried a second experiment to address these questions. Everything was the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(same except that in each initial population all)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( t)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( genes were set to 0 and the frequency of)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( p)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( = 1 was 0.7. The)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( t)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( =)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(1 alleles could be discovered only by mutation. Collins and Jefferson found that once)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( t)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( = 1 alleles had)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(accumulated to approximately half the population \(which took about 100 generations\), they quickly took over)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the population \(frequency > 0.9\), and)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( p)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( = 1 increased from 0.7 to approximately 0.8. This indicates, in a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(simple model, the power of sexual selection even in the face of negative natural selection for a trait. It also)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(shows very clearly how, above some threshold frequency, the "invasion" of the trait into the population can)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(take place at an accelerating rate, and how the system can get caught in a feedback loop between frequency of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the trait in males and preference for the trait in females in the manner of Fisher's runaway sexual selection.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Collins and Jefferson performed additional experiments in which other assumptions were relaxed. In one)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(experiment the choice of mates not only depended on)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( T)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( but was also constrained by spatial distance \(again)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(more realistic than Kirkpatrick's original model, since in most populations organisms do not mate with others)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(living far distances away\); in another the organisms were diploid instead of haploid and contained "dominant")Tj
0 -13.2 Td(and "recessive" alleles. Both these variations are difficult to treat analytically. Collins and Jefferson found that)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(both variations led to dynamics significantly different from those of Kirkpatrick's original model. In one)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(simulation with diploid organisms, a)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( t)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( = 1 allele not initially present in large numbers in the population was)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(unable to invade\227its frequency remained close to 0 for 1000 generations. However, when mating was)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(constrained spatially, the)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( t)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( = 1 allele was able to slowly invade the population to the point where significant)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(sexual selection could take place.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(These examples show that relaxing some of the simplifying assumptions in idealized mathematical models)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(can dramatically change the behavior of the system. One benefit of Collins and Jefferson's simulation was to)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(show in which ways the original analytic model does not capture the behavior of more realistic versions. It)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(also allowed Collins and Jefferson to study the behavior and dynamics of these more realistic versions,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(particularly at points away from equilibrium. Another benefit of such models is that they allow scientists to)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(systematically vary parts of the model to discover which forces are most important in changing behavior. It is)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(clear that Collins and Jefferson's simulations do not go far enough in realism, but computer models are)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(inching in that direction. Of course, as was pointed out earlier, the more realistic the model, the more)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(computationally expensive it becomes and the harder it is to analyze the results. At some point, the realism of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(a model can override its usefulness, since studying it would be no more enlightening than studying the actual)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(system in nature. It is the art of effective modeling to strike the proper balance between simplicity \(which)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(makes understanding possible\) and generality \(which ensures that the results are meaningful\).)Tj
/F9 15.8 Tf 0 -57.64 Td(3.3 MODELING ECOSYSTEMS)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -27.368 Td(In the real world, evolution takes place not in populations of independent organisms \(such as our populations)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(of evolving cellular automata described in chapter 2\) but in ecologies of interacting organisms. Ecological)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(interactions have been captured to varying degrees in some of the case studies we have considered, such as the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Prisoner's Dilemma project \(where the evolving strategies played against one another\), the sorting networks)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(project \(where hosts and parasites were in direct competition\), and the Evolutionary Reinforcement Learning)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(\(ERL\) project \(where the evolving agents competed indirectly for the available food\). Such interactions,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(however, are only the faintest shadow of the complexity of interactions in real-world ecologies. A more)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(ambitious model of evolution in an ecological setting is Echo, first conceived of and implemented by John)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Holland \(1975, second edition, chapter 10; see also Holland 1994\) and later reimplemented and extended by)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Terry Jones and Stephanie Forrest \(Jones and Forrest 1993; see also Forrest and Jones 1994\).)Tj
/F8 11 Tf 121.235 685.608 Td(Chapter 3: Genetic Algorithms in Scientific Models)Tj
