/F4 11 Tf 0 -27.368 Td(The words "evolution" and "adaptation" have been used throughout this book \(and in most books about)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(evolution\) with little more than informal definition. But if these are phenomena of central scientific interest, it)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(is important to define them in a more rigorous and quantitative way, and to develop methods to detect and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(measure them. In other words: How can we decide if an observed system is evolving? How can we measure)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the rate of evolution in such a system?)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Mark Bedau and Norman Packard \(1992\) developed a measure of evolution, called "evolutionary activity," to)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(address these questions. Bedau and Packard point out that evolution is more than "sustained change" or even)Tj
0 -13.2 Td("sustained complex change"; it is "the spontaneous generation of innovative functional structures." These)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(structures are designed and continually modified by the evolutionary process; they persist because of their)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(adaptive functionality. The goal, then, is to find a way to measure the degree to which a system is)Tj
0 -13.2 Td("continuously and spontaneously generating adaptations.")Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Bedau and Packard assert that "persistent usage of new genes is what signals genuine evolutionary activity,")Tj
0 -13.2 Td(since evolutionary activity is meant to measure the degree to which useful new genes are discovered and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(persist in the population. The "use" of a gene or combination of genes is not simply its presence in a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(chromosome; it must be used to produce some trait or behavior. Assigning credit to particular genes for a trait)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(or behavior is notoriously hard because of the complex interconnection of gene activities in the formation and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(control of an organism. However, Bedau and Packard believe that this can be usefully done in some contexts.)Tj
0 -39.6 Td(Bedau and Packard's first attempt at measuring evolutionary activity was in an idealized computer model,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(called "Strategic Bugs," in which gene use was easy to measure. Their model was similar to, though simpler)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(than, the ERL model described above. The Strategic Bugs world is a simulated two-dimensional lattice)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(containing only "bugs" and "food." The food supply is refreshed periodically and is distributed randomly)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(across the lattice. Bugs survive by finding food and storing it in an internal reservoir until they have enough)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(energy to reproduce. Bugs also use energy from their internal reservoir in order to move, and they are "taxed")Tj
0 -13.2 Td(energy just for surviving from time step to time step even if they do not move. A bug dies when its internal)Tj
