0 -26.4 Td(Holland frames adaptation as a tension between "exploration" \(the search for new, useful adaptations\) and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td("exploitation" \(the use and propagation of these adaptations\). The tension comes about since any move toward)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(exploration\227testing previously unseen schemas or schemas whose instances seen so far have low)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(fitness\227takes away from the exploitation of tried and true schemas. In any system \(e.g., a population of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(organisms\) required to face environments with some degree of unpredictability, an optimal balance between)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(exploration and exploitation must be found. The system has to keep trying out new possibilities \(or else it)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(could "overadapt" and be inflexible in the face of novelty\), but it also has to continually incorporate and use)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(past experience as a guide for future behavior.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Holland's original GA was proposed as an "adaptive plan" for accomplishing a proper balance between)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(exploration and exploitation in)Tj
( an adaptive system. \(In this chapter, "GA" will generally refer to Holland's)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(original GA, which is essentially the "simple" GA that I described in chapter 1 above.\))Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Holland's schema analysis demonstrated that, given certain assumptions, the GA indeed achieves a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(near-optimal balance. Holland's arguments for this are based on an analogy with the Two-Armed Bandit)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(problem, whose solution is sketched below.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Holland's original theory of schemas assumed binary strings and single-point crossover. Useful schemas, as)Tj
