0 -13.2 Td(proceeds from low-order partitions at early times to higher-order partitions at later times. The extent to which)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(this describes the workings of actual GAs is not clear. Grefenstette \(1991b\) gives some further caveats)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(concerning this description of GA behavior. In particular, he points out that, at each new generation, selection)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(most likely produces a new set of biases in the way schemas are sampled \(as in the fitness function given by)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(equation 4.5 above\). Since the Schema Theorem gives the dynamics of schema sampling over only one time)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(step, it is difficult \(if not impossible\), without making unrealistic assumptions, to make any long-range)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(prediction about how the allocation of trials to different schemas will proceed. Because of the biases)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(introduced by selection, the static average fitnesses of schemas will not necessarily be correlated in any useful)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(way with their observed average fitnesses.)Tj
/F9 13.2 Tf 0 -28.6 Td(Implications for GA Performance)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -26.84 Td(In view of the solution to the Two-Armed Bandit problem, the exponential allocation of trials is clearly an)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(appropriate strategy for problems in)Tj
( which "on-line" performance is important, such as real-time control)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(problems. Less clear is its appropriateness for problems with other performance criteria, such as "Is the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(optimal individual reliably discovered in a reasonable time?" There have been many cases in the GA literature)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(in which such criteria have been applied, sometimes leading to the conclusion that GAs don't work. This)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(demonstrates the importance of understanding what a particular algorithm is good at doing before applying it)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(to particular problems. This point was made eloquently in the context of GAs by De Jong \(1993\).)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(What is maximizing on-line performance good for? Holland's view is clear: the rough maximization of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(on-line performance is what goes on in adaptive systems of all kinds, and in some sense this is how)Tj
0 -13.2 Td("adaptation" is defined. In the realm of technology, on-line performance is important in control problems)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(\(e.g., automatically controlling machinery\) and in learning problems \(e.g., learning to navigate in an)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(environment\) in which each system action can lead to a gain or loss. It is also important in prediction tasks)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(\(e.g., predicting financial markets\) in which gains or losses are had with each prediction. Most of the GA)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(applications I discussed in chapter 2 had a slightly different performance criterion: to find a "reasonably good")Tj
0 -13.2 Td(\(if not perfect\) solution in a reasonable amount of time. In other words, the goal is to)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( satisfice)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( \(find a solution)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(that is good enough for one's purposes\) rather than to optimize \(find the best possible solution\). This goal is)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(related to maximizing on-line performance, since on-line performance will be maximized if high-fitness)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(individuals are likely to be chosen at each step, including the last. The two-armed-bandit allocation strategy)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(maximizes both cumulative payoff and the amount of information the gambler gets for her money as to which)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(is the better arm. In the context of schemas, the exponential allocation strategy could be said to maximize, for)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(a given amount of time \(samples\), the amount of information gained about which part of the search space is)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(likely to provide good solutions if sampled in the future. Gaining such information is crucial to successful)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(satisficing. For true optimization, hybrid methods such as a GA augmented by a hill climber or other kinds of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(gradient search have often been found to perform better than a GA alone \(see, e.g., Hart and Belew 1996\).)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(GAs seem to be good at quickly finding promising regions of the search space, and hill climbers are often)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(good at zeroing in on optimal individuals in those regions.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Although the foregoing schema analysis has suggested the types of problems for which a genetic algorithm)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(might be useful, it does not answer more detailed questions, such as "Given a certain version of the GA, what)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(is the expected time to find a particular fitness level?" Some approaches to such questions will be described)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(below.)Tj
/F8 11 Tf 103.19 656.04 Td(Chapter 4: Theoretical Foundations of Genetic Algorithms)Tj
