/F9 13.2 Tf 0 684.8 Td 0.000 Tc(Deceiving a Genetic Algorithm)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -26.84 Td(The theory of schemas has been used by some in the GA community to propose an answer to "What makes a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(problem hard for a GA?" As described above, the view is that competition among schemas roughly proceeds)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(from low-order schema partitions at early times to higher-order schema partitions at later times. Bethke)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(\(1980\) reasoned that it will be hard for a GA to find the optimum of a fitness function if low-order partitions)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(contain misleading information about higher-order partitions. The following extreme example illustrates this.)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Call a schema)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( H)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( a "winner" if its static average fitness is highest in its partition. Suppose that any schema)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(whose defined bits are all ones is a winner except for the length)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( L)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( schema 1111\267\267\2671, and let 0000\267\267\2670 be a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(winner. In principle, it should be hard for a GA to find 0000\267\267\2670, since every lower-order partition gives)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(misleading information about where the optimum is likely to be found. Such a fitness function is termed "fully)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(deceptive." \(The term "deceptive" was introduced by Goldberg \(1987\).\) Fitness functions with lesser amounts)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(of deception are also possible \(i.e., some partitions give correct information about the location of the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(optimum\). Bethke used "Walsh Transforms"\227similar to Fourier transforms\227to design fitness functions with)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(various degrees of deception. For reviews of this work, see Goldberg 1989b,c and Forrest and Mitchell 1993a.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Subsequent to Bethke's work, Goldberg and his colleagues carried out a number of theoretical studies of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(deception in fitness functions, and deception has become a central focus of theoretical work on GAs. \(See,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(e.g., Das and Whitley 1991; Deb and Goldberg 1993; Goldberg 1989c; Liepins and Vose 1990, 1991; Whitley)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(1991.\))Tj
0 -26.4 Td(It should be noted that the study of GA deception is generally concerned with function optimization; the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(deception in a fitness function is assumed to make it difficult to find a global optimum. In view of the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(widespread use of GAs as function optimizers, deception can be an important factor for GA practitioners to)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(understand. However, if one takes the view that GAs are not function optimizers but rather "satisficers" or)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(payoff maximizers, then deception may be less of a concern. As De Jong \(1993, p. 15\) puts it, the GA \(as)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(formulated by Holland\) "is attempting to maximize)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( cumulative)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( payoff from arbitrary landscapes, deceptive or)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(otherwise. In general, this is achieved by not investing too much effort in finding cleverly hidden peaks \(the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(risk/reward ratio is too high\).")Tj
/F9 13.2 Tf 0 -28.6 Td(Limitations of "Static" Schema Analysis)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -26.84 Td(A number of recent papers have questioned the relevance of schema analysis to the understanding of real GAs)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(\(e.g., Grefenstette 1993; Mason 1993; Peck and Dhawan 1993\). Here I will focus on Grefenstette's critique of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the "Static Building Block Hypothesis.")Tj
0 -39.6 Td(The following qualitative formulation of the Schema Theorem and the Building Block Hypothesis should now)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(be familiar to the reader: The simple GA increases the number of instances of low-order,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(short-defininglength, high-observed-fitness schemas via the multi-armed-bandit strategy, and these)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(schemas serve as building blocks that are combined, via crossover, into candidate solutions with increasingly)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(higher order and higher observed fitness. The rationale for this strategy is based on the assumption that the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(observed and static fitnesses of schemas are correlated; some potential problems with this assumption have)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(been pointed out in the previous sections.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Grefenstette \(1993, p. 78\) claims that much work on GA theory has assumed a stronger version that he calls)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the "Static Building Block Hypothesis" \(SBBH\): "Given any low-order, short-defining-length hyperplane)Tj
0 -13.2 Td([i.e., schema] partition, a GA is expected to converge to the hyperplane [in that partition] with the best static)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(average fitness \(the 'expected winner'\)." This is stronger than the original formulation, since it states that the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(GA will converge on the)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( actual)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( winners of each short, low-order partition competition rather than on the)Tj
/F8 11 Tf 103.19 685.08 Td(Chapter 4: Theoretical Foundations of Genetic Algorithms)Tj
