/F4 11 Tf 0 687 Td 0.000 Tc(schemas with the best)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( observed)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( fitness. The SBBH was not what Holland \(1975\) proposed, and it has never)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(been proved or even empirically validated, but it implicitly underlies the assumption that deceptive fitness)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(functions will be difficult for a GA.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Grefenstette gives two possible reasons, related to his earlier concerns about the two-armed-bandit analogy,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(why the SBBH can fail:)Tj
/F5 11 Tf 0 -26.4 Td(Collateral convergence)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( Once the population begins to converge at some loci, the samples of some schemas)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(are no longer uniform. For example, suppose instances of 111 *\267\267\267* are highly fit and the population has more)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(or less converged on those bits \(i.e., nearly every member of the population is an instance of that schema\).)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Then almost all samples of, say ***000 *\267\267\267* will actually be samples of 111000 *\267\267\267*. This may prevent the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(GA from making any accurate estimate of)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( u)Tj
/F4 11 Tf (\(* * *000 *\267\267\267*\).)Tj
/F5 11 Tf 0 -26.4 Td(High fitness variance)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( If a schema's static average fitness has high variance, the GA may not be able to make)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(an accurate estimate of this static average fitness. The fitness function given by equation 4.5 is an example of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(this: the variance of 1 *\267\267\267* is high, so the GA converges on the highfitness subregions of it. As before, this)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(biases all subsequent samples of this schema, preventing an accurate estimate of its static fitness.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(These points bear directly on the relevance of deception to the behavior of GAs, since deceptive fitness)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(functions are defined entirely in terms of the static average fitnesses of schemas. To illustrate the problems)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(with this, Grefenstette gives examples of deceptive problems that are easy for GAs to optimize and of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(nondeceptive problems that are arbitrarily hard)Tj
( for GAs to optimize. He concludes that deception is neither)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(necessary nor sufficient to cause difficulties for GAs, and that its relevance to the study of GAs remains to be)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(demonstrated.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(There is nothing to indicate that the features listed above harm search performance of GAs; they only)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(demonstrate the danger of drawing conclusions about the expected behavior of GAs from the static average)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(fitnesses of schemas. Instead, a more dynamic approach is needed that takes into account the biases)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(introduced by selection at each generation. Such approaches are described in the next several sections.)Tj
/F9 15.8 Tf 0 -57.64 Td(4.2 ROYAL ROADS)Tj
/F9 13.2 Tf 0 -42.768 Td(Royal Road Functions)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -26.84 Td(The Schema Theorem, by itself, addresses the positive effects of selection \(allocating increasing samples of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(schemas with observed high performance\) but only the negative aspects of crossover\227that is, the extent to)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(which it disrupts schemas. It does not address the question of how crossover works to recombine highly fit)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(schemas, even though this is seen by many as the major source of the search power of genetic algorithms. The)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Building Block Hypothesis states that crossover combines short, observed high-performance schemas into)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(increasingly fit candidate solutions, but does not give any detailed description of how this combination)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(To investigate schema processing and recombination in more detail, Stephanie Forrest, John Holland, and I)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(designed a class of fitness landscapes, called Royal Road functions, that were meant to capture the essence of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(building blocks in an idealized form \(Mitchell, Forrest, and Holland 1992; Forrest and Mitchell 1993b;)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Mitchell, Holland, and Forrest 1994\).)Tj
