/F4 11 Tf 0 -27.368 Td(As for any search and learning method, the way in which candidate solutions are encoded is a central, if not)Tj
/F6 11 Tf 0 -13.2 Td(the)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( central, factor in the success of a genetic algorithm. Most GA applications use fixed-length, fixed-order)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(bit strings to encode candidate solutions. However, in recent years, there have been many experiments with)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(other kinds of encodings, several of which were described in previous chapters.)Tj
/F9 13.2 Tf 0 -28.6 Td(Binary Encodings)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -26.84 Td(Binary encodings \(i.e., bit strings\) are the most common encodings for a number of reasons. One is historical:)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(in their earlier work, Holland and his students concentrated on such encodings and GA practice has)Tj
( tended to)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(follow this lead. Much of the existing GA theory is based on the assumption of fixed-length, fixed-order)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(binary encodings. Much of that theory can be extended to apply to nonbinary encodings, but such extensions)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(are not as well developed as the original theory. In addition, heuristics about appropriate parameter settings)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(\(e.g., for crossover and mutation rates\) have generally been developed in the context of binary encodings.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(There have been many extensions to the basic binary encoding schema, such as gray coding \(Bethke 1980;)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Caruana and Schaffer 1988\) and Hillis's diploid binary encoding scheme. \(Diploid encodings were actually)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(first proposed in Holland 1975, and are also discussed in Goldberg 1989a.\))Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Holland \(1975\) gave a theoretical justification for using binary encodings. He compared two encodings with)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(roughly the same information-carrying capacity, one with a small number of alleles and long strings \(e.g., bit)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(strings of length 100\) and the other with a large number of alleles and short strings \(e.g., decimal strings of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(length 30\). He argued that the former allows for a higher degree of implicit parallelism than the latter, since an)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(instance of the former contains more schemas than an instance of the latter \(2)Tj
/F4 7.6 Tf 340.615 5.042 Td(100)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 11.458 -5.042 Td( versus 2)Tj
/F4 7.6 Tf 39.105 5.042 Td(30)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 7.639 -5.042 Td(\). \(This)Tj
-398.817 -13.2 Td(schema-counting argument is relevant to GA behavior only insofar as schema analysis is relevant, which, as I)Tj
