/F9 13.2 Tf 0 -28.6 Td(Many-Character and Real-Valued Encodings)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -26.84 Td(For many applications, it is most natural to use an alphabet of many characters or real numbers to form)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(chromosomes. Examples include Kitano's many-character representation for graph-generation grammars,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Meyer and Packard's real-valued representation for condition sets, Montana and Davis's real-valued)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(representation for neural-network weights, and Schultz-Kremer's real-valued representation for torsion)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(angles in proteins.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Holland's schema-counting argument seems to imply that GAs should exhibit worse performance on)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(multiple-character encodings than on binary encodings. However, this has been questioned by some \(see,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(e.g., Antonisse 1989\). Several empirical comparisons between binary encodings and multiple-character or)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(real-valued encodings have shown better performance for the latter \(see, e.g., Janikow and Michalewicz)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(1991; Wright 1991\). But the performance depends very much on the problem and the details of the GA being)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(used, and at present there are no rigorous guidelines for predicting which encoding will work best.)Tj
/F9 13.2 Tf 0 -28.6 Td(Tree Encodings)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -26.84 Td(Tree encoding schemes, such as John Koza's scheme for representing computer programs, have several)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(advantages, including the fact that they allow the search space to be open-ended \(in principle, any size tree)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(could be formed via crossover and mutation\). This open-endedness also leads to some potential pitfalls. The)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(trees can grow large in uncontrolled ways, preventing the formation of more structured, hierarchical candidate)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(solutions. \(Koza's \(1992, 1994\) "automatic definition of functions" is one way in which GP can be encouraged)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(to design hierarchically structured programs.\) Also, the resulting trees, being large, can be very difficult to)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(understand and to simplify. Systematic experiments evaluating the usefulness of tree encodings and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(comparing them with other encodings are only just beginning in the genetic programming community.)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Likewise, as yet there are only very nascent attempts at extending GA theory to tree encodings \(see, e.g.,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Tackett 1994; O'Reilly and Oppacher 1995\).)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(These are only the most common encodings; a survey of the GA literature will turn up experiments on several)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(others.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(How is one to decide on the correct encoding for one's problem? Lawrence Davis, a researcher with much)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(experience applying GAs to realworld problems, strongly advocates using whatever encoding is the most)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(natural for your problem, and then devising a GA that can use that encoding \(Davis 1991\). Until the theory of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(GAs and encodings is better formulated, this might be the best philosophy; as can be seen from the examples)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(presented in this book, most research is currently done by guessing at an appropriate encoding and then trying)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(out a particular version of the GA on it. This is not much different from other areas of machine learning; for)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(example, encoding a learning problem for a neural net is typically done by trial and error.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(One appealing idea is to have the encoding itself adapt so that the GA can make better use of it.)Tj
/F9 15.8 Tf 0 -57.64 Td(5.3 ADAPTING THE ENCODING)Tj
