/F4 11 Tf 0 -26.84 Td(Most GAs described in the literature have been "generational"\227at each generation the new population)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(consists entirely of offspring formed by parents in the previous generation \(though some of these offspring)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(may be identical to their parents\). In some schemes, such as the elitist schemes described above, successive)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(generations overlap to some degree\227some portion of the previous generation is retained in the new)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(population. The fraction of new individuals at each generation has been called the "generation gap" \(De Jong)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(1975\). In steady-state selection, only a few individuals are replaced in each generation: usually a small)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(number of the least fit individuals are replaced by offspring resulting from crossover and mutation of the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(fittest individuals. Steady-state GAs are often used in evolving rule-based systems \(e.g., classifier systems;)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(see Holland 1986\) in which incremental learning \(and remembering what has already been learned\) is)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(important and in which members of the population collectively \(rather than individually\) solve the problem at)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(hand. Steady-state selection has been analyzed by Syswerda \(1989, 1991\), by Whitley \(1989\), and by De)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Jong and Sarma \(1993\).)Tj
