0 -13.2 Td(the simplest form: a single cross)Tj
(over position is chosen at random and the parts of two parents after the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(crossover position are exchanged to form two offspring. The idea here is, of course, to recombine building)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(blocks \(schemas\) on different strings. Single-point crossover has some shortcomings, though. For one thing,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(it cannot combine all possible schemas. For example, it cannot in general combine instances of 11*****1 and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(****11** to form an instance of 11**11*1. Likewise, schemas with long defining lengths are likely to be)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(destroyed under single-point crossover. Eshelman, Caruana, and Schaffer \(1989\) call this "positional bias":)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the schemas that can be created or destroyed by a crossover depend strongly on the location of the bits in the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(chromosome. Single-point crossover assumes that short, low-order schemas are the functional building)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(blocks of strings, but one generally does not know in advance what ordering of bits will group functionally)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(related bits together\227this was the purpose of the inversion operator and other adaptive operators described)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(above. Eshelman, Caruana, and Schaffer also point out that there may not be any way to put all functionally)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(related bits close together on a string, since particular bits might be crucial in more than one schema. They)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(point out further that the tendency of single-point crossover to keep short schemas intact can lead to the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(preservation of hitchhikers\227bits that are not part of a desired schema but which, by being close on the string,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(hitchhike along with the beneficial schema as it reproduces. \(This was seen in the "Royal Road" experiments,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(described above in chapter 4.\) Many people have also noted that singlepoint crossover treats some loci)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(preferentially: the segments exchanged between the two parents always contain the endpoints of the strings.)Tj
/F8 11 Tf 103.19 677.688 Td(Chapter 4: Theoretical Foundations of Genetic Algorithms)Tj
