/F4 11 Tf 0 687 Td 0.000 Tc(To reduce positional bias and this "endpoint" effect, many GA practitioners use two-point crossover, in which)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(two positions are chosen at random and the segments between them are exchanged. Two-point crossover is)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(less likely to disrupt schemas with large defining lengths and can combine more schemas than single-point)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(crossover. In addition, the segments that are exchanged do not necessarily contain the endpoints of the strings.)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Again, there are schemas that two-point crossover cannot combine. GA practitioners have experimented with)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(different numbers of crossover points \(in one method, the number of crossover points for each pair of parents)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(is chosen from a Poisson distribution whose mean is a function of the length of the chromosome\). Some)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(practitioners \(e.g., Spears and De Jong \(1991\)\) believe strongly in the superiority of "parameterized uniform)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(crossover," in which an exchange happens at each bit position with probability)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( p)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( \(typically 0.5 d)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( p)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( d 0.8\).)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Parameterized uniform crossover has no positional bias\227any schemas contained at different positions in the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(parents can potentially be recombined in the offspring. However, this lack of positional bias can prevent)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(coadapted alleles from ever forming in the population, since parameterized uniform crossover can be highly)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(disruptive of any schema.)Tj
0 -39.6 Td(Given these \(and the many other variants of crossover found in the GA literature\), which one should you use?)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(There is no simple answer; the success or failure of a particular crossover operator depends in complicated)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(ways on the particular fitness function, encoding, and other details of the GA. It is still a very important open)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(problem to fully understand these interactions. There are many papers in the GA literature quantifying aspects)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(of various crossover operators \(positional bias, disruption potential, ability to create different schemas in one)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(step, and so on\), but these do not give definitive guidance on when to use which type of crossover. There are)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(also many papers in which the usefulness of different types of crossover is empirically compared, but all these)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(studies rely on particular small suites of test functions, and different studies produce conflicting results.)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Again, it is hard to glean general conclusions. It is common in recent GA applications to use either two-point)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(crossover or parameterized uniform crossover with)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( p)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( H 0.7\2260.8.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(For the most part, the comments and references above deal with crossover in the context of bit-string)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(encodings, though some of them apply to other types of encodings as well. Some types of encodings require)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(specially defined crossover and mutation operators\227for example, the tree encoding used in genetic)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(programming, or encodings for problems like the Traveling Salesman problem \(in which the task is to find a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(correct ordering for a collection of objects\).)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Most of the comments above also assume that crossover's ability to recombine highly fit schemas is the reason)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(it should be useful. Given some of the challenges we have seen to the relevance of schemas as a analysis tool)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(for understanding GAs, one might ask if we should not consider the possibility that crossover is actually)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(useful for some entirely different reason \(e.g., it is in essence a "macro-mutation" operator that simply allows)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(for large jumps in the search space\). I must leave this question as an open area of GA research for interested)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(readers to explore. \(Terry Jones \(1995\) has performed some interesting, though preliminary, experiments)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(attempting to tease out the different possible roles of crossover in GAs.\) Its answer might also shed light on)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the question of why recombination is useful for real organisms \(if indeed it is\)\227a controversial and still open)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(question in evolutionary biology.)Tj
/F9 13.2 Tf 0 -28.6 Td(Mutation)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -26.84 Td(A common view in the GA community, dating back to Holland's book)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( Adaptation in Natural and ARtificial)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Systems)Tj
/F4 11 Tf (, is that crossover is the major instrument of variation and innovation in GAs, with mutation insuring)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the population against permanent fixation at any particular locus and thus playing more of a background role.)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(This differs from the traditional positions of other evolutionary computation methods, such as evolutionary)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(programming and early versions of evolution strategies, in which random mutation is the only source of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(variation. \(Later versions of evolution strategies have included a form of crossover.\))Tj
/F8 11 Tf 103.19 682.44 Td(Chapter 4: Theoretical Foundations of Genetic Algorithms)Tj
