/F4 11 Tf 0 687 Td 0.000 Tc(However, the appreciation of the role of mutation is growing as the GA community attempts to understand)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(how GAs solve complex problems. Some comparative studies have been performed on the power of mutation)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(versus crossover; for example, Spears \(1993\) formally verified the intuitive idea that, while mutation and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(crossover have the same ability for "disruption" of existing schemas, crossover is a more robust "constructor")Tj
0 -13.2 Td(of new schemas. M\374hlenbein \(1992, p. 15\), on the other hand, argues that in many cases a hill-climbing)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(strategy will work better than a GA with crossover and that "the power of mutation has been underestimated)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(in traditional genetic algorithms." As we saw in the Royal Road experiments in chapter 4, it is not a choice)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(between crossover or mutation but rather the balance among crossover, mutation, and selection that is all)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(important. The correct balance also depends on details of the fitness function and the encoding. Furthermore,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(crossover and mutation vary in relative usefulness over the course of a run. Precisely how all this happens still)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(needs to be elucidated. In my opinion, the most promising prospect for producing the right balances over the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(course of a run is to find ways for the GA to adapt its own mutation and crossover rates during a search. Some)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(attempts at this will be described below.)Tj
/F9 13.2 Tf 0 -28.6 Td(Other Operators and Mating Strategies)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -26.84 Td(Though most GA applications use only crossover and mutation, many other operators and strategies for)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(applying them have been explored in the GA literature. These include inversion and gene doubling \(discussed)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(above\) and several operators for preserving diversity in the population. For example, De Jong \(1975\))Tj
0 -13.2 Td(experimented with a "crowding" operator in which a newly formed offspring replaced the existing individual)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(most similar to itself. This prevented too many similar individuals \("crowds"\) from being in the population at)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the same time. Goldberg and Richardson \(1987\) accomplished a similar result using an explicit "fitness)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(sharing" function: each individual's fitness was decreased by the presence of other population members,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(where the amount of decrease due to each other population member was an explicit increasing function of the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(similarity between the two individuals. Thus, individuals that were similar to many other individuals were)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(punished, and individuals that were different were rewarded. Goldberg and Richardson showed that in some)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(cases this could induce appropriate "speciation," allowing the population members to converge on several)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(peaks in the fitness landscape rather than all converging to the same peak. Smith, Forrest, and Perelson \(1993\))Tj
0 -13.2 Td(showed that a similar effect could be obtained without the presence of an explicit sharing function.)Tj
0 -39.6 Td(A different way to promote diversity is to put restrictions on mating. For example, if only sufficiently similar)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(individuals are allowed to mate, distinct "species" \(mating groups\) will tend to form. This approach has been)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(studied by Deb and Goldberg \(1989\). Eshelman \(1991\) and Eshelman and Schaffer \(1991\) used the opposite)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(tack: they disallowed matings between sufficiently similar individuals \("incest"\). Their desire was not to form)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(species but rather to keep the entire population as diverse as possible. Holland \(1975\) and Booker \(1985\) have)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(suggested using "mating tags"\227parts of the chromosome that identify prospective mates to one another. Only)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(those individuals with matching tags are allowed to mate \(a kind of "sexual selection" procedure\). These tags)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(would, in principle, evolve along with the rest of the chromosome to adaptively implement appropriate)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(restrictions on mating. Finally, there have been some experiments with spatially restricted mating \(see, e.g.,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Hillis 1992\): the population evolves on a spatial lattice, and individuals are likely to mate only with)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(individuals in their spatial neighborhoods. Hillis found that such a scheme helped preserve diversity by)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(maintaining spatially isolated species, with innovations largely occurring at the boundaries between species.)Tj
/F9 15.8 Tf 0 -57.64 Td(5.6 PARAMETERS FOR GENETIC ALGORITHMS)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -27.368 Td(The fourth decision to make in implementing a genetic algorithm is how to set the values for the various)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(parameters, such as population size, crossover rate, and mutation rate. These parameters typically interact with)Tj
/F8 11 Tf 103.19 688.248 Td(Chapter 4: Theoretical Foundations of Genetic Algorithms)Tj
