/F4 11 Tf 0 687 Td 0.000 Tc(one another nonlinearly, so they cannot be optimized one at a time. There is a great deal of discussion of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(parameter settings and approaches to parameter adaptation in the evolutionary computation literature\227too)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(much to survey or even list here. There are no conclusive results on what is best;most people use what has)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(worked well in previously reported cases. Here I will review some of the experimental approaches people)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(have taken to find the "best" parameter settings.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(De Jong \(1975\) performed an early systematic study of how varying parameters affected the GA's on-line and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(off-line search performance on a small suite of test functions. Recall from chapter 4, thought exercise 3, that)Tj
0 -13.2 Td("on-line" performance at time)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( t)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( is the average fitness of all the individuals that have been evaluated over)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( t)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -13.2 Td(evaluation steps. The off-line performance at time)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( t)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( is the average value, over)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( t)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( evaluation steps, of the best)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(fitness that has been seen up to each evaluation step. De Jong's experiments indicated that the best population)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(size was 50\226100 individuals, the best single-point crossover rate was ~0.6 per pair of parents, and the best)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(mutation rate was 0.001 per bit. These settings \(along with De Jong's test suite\) became widely used in the GA)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(community, even though it was not clear how well the GA would perform with these settings on problems)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(outside De Jong's test suite. Any guidance was gratefully accepted.)Tj
0 -39.6 Td(Somewhat later, Grefenstette \(1986\) noted that, since the GA could be used as an optimization procedure, it)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(could be used to optimize the parameters for another GA! \(A similar study was done by Bramlette \(1991\).\) In)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Grefenstette's experiments, the "meta-level GA" evolved a population of 50 GA parameter sets for the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(problems in De Jong's test suite. Each individual encoded six GA parameters: population size, crossover rate,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(mutation rate, generation gap, scaling window \(a particular scaling technique that I won't discuss here\), and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(selection strategy \(elitist or nonelitist\). The fitness of an individual was a function of the on-line or off-line)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(performance of a GA using the parameters encoded by that individual. The meta-level GA itself used De)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Jong's parameter settings. The fittest individual for on-line performance set the population size to 30, the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(crossover rate to 0.95, the mutation rate to 0.01, and the generation gap to 1, and used elitist selection. These)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(parameters gave a small but significant improvement in on-line performance over De Jong's settings. Notice)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(that Grefenstette's results call for a smaller population and higher crossover and mutation rates than De Jong's.)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(The meta-level GA was not able to find a parameter set that beat De Jong's for off-line performance. This)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(was an interesting experiment, but again, in view of the specialized test suite, it is not clear how generally)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(these recommendations hold. Others have shown that there are many fitness functions for which these)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(parameter settings are not optimal.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Schaffer, Caruana, Eshelman, and Das \(1989\) spent over a year of CPU time systematically testing a wide)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(range of parameter combinations. The performance of a parameter set was the on-line performance of a GA)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(with those parameters on a small set of numerical optimization problems \(including some of De Jong's)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(functions\) encoded with gray coding. Schaffer et al. found that the best settings for population size, crossover)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(rate, and mutation rate were independent of the problem in their test suite. These settings were similar to those)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(found by Grefenstette:population size 20\22630, crossover rate 0.75\2260.95, and mutation rate 0.005\2260.01. It may)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(be surprising that a very small population size was better, especially in light of other studies that have argued)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(for larger population sizes \(e.g., Goldberg 1989d\), but this may be due to the on-line performance measure:)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(since each individual ever evaluated contributes to the on-line performance, there is a large cost for)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(evaluating a large population.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Although Grefenstette and Schaffer et al. found that a particular setting of parameters worked best for on-line)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(performance on their test suites, it seems unlikely that any general principles about parameter settings can be)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(formulated)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( a priori)Tj
/F4 11 Tf (, in view of the variety of problem types, encodings, and performance criteria that are)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(possible in different applications. Moreover, the optimal population size, crossover rate, and mutation rate)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(likely change over the course of a single run. Many people feel that the)Tj
( most promising approach is to have)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the parameter values)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( adapt)Tj
/F4 11 Tf ( in real time to the ongoing search. There have been several approaches to)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(selfadaptation of GA parameters. For example, this has long been a focus of research in the evolution)Tj
/F8 11 Tf 103.19 693 Td(Chapter 4: Theoretical Foundations of Genetic Algorithms)Tj
