/F9 19 Tf 0 678.992 Td 0.000 Tc(Chapter 6: Conclusions and Future Directions)Tj
/F9 15.8 Tf 0 -32.842 Td(Overview)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -27.368 Td(In this book we have seen that genetic algorithms can be a powerful tool for solving problems and for)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(simulating natural systems in a wide variety of scientific fields. In examining the accomplishments of these)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(algorithms, we have also seen that many unanswered questions remain. It is now time to summarize what the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(field of genetic algorithms has achieved, and what are the most interesting and important directions for future)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(research.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(From the case studies of projects in problem-solving, scientific modeling, and theory we can draw the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(following conclusions:)Tj
/Fc 11 Tf 28.19 -26.4 Td(\267 )Tj
/F4 11 Tf 7.81 -13.2 Td(GAs are promising methods for solving difficult technological problems, and for machine learning.)Tj
-0 -13.2 Td(More generally, GAs are part of a new movement in computer science that is exploring biologically)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(inspired approaches to computation. Advocates of this movement believe that in order to create the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(kinds of computing systems we need\227systems that are adaptable, massively parallel, able to deal)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(with complexity, able to learn, and even creative\227we should copy natural systems with these)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(qualities. Natural evolution is a particularly appealing source of inspiration.)Tj
/Fc 11 Tf -7.81 -26.4 Td(\267 )Tj
/F4 11 Tf 7.81 -13.2 Td(Genetic algorithms are also promising approaches for modeling the natural systems that inspired their)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(design. Most models using GAs are meant to be "gedanken experiments" or "idea models")Tj
-0 -13.2 Td(\(Roughgarden et al. 1996\) rather than precise simulations attempting to match real-world data. The)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(purposes of these idea models are to make ideas precise and to test their plausibility by implementing)Tj
-0 -13.2 Td(them as computer programs \(e.g., Hinton and Nowlan's model of the Baldwin effect\), to understand)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(and predict general tendencies of natural systems \(e.g., Echo\), and to see how these tendencies are)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(affected by changes in details of the model \(e.g., Collins and Jefferson's variations on Kirkpatrick's)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(sexual selection model\). These models can allow scientists to perform experiments that would not be)Tj
-0 -13.2 Td(possible in the real world, and to simulate phenomena that are difficult or impossible to capture and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(analyze in a set of equations. These models also have a largely unexplored but potentially interesting)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(side that has not so far been mentioned here: by explicitly modeling)Tj
( evolution as a computer program,)Tj
-0 -13.2 Td(we explicitly cast evolution as a computational process, and thus we can think about it in this new)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(light. For example, we can attempt to measure the "information" contained in a population and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(attempt to understand exactly how evolution processes that information to create structures that lead)Tj
-0 -13.2 Td(to higher fitness. Such a computational view, made concrete by GA-type computer models, will, I)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(believe, eventually be an essential part of understanding the relationships among evolution,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(information theory, and the creation and adaptation of organization in biological systems \(e.g., see)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Weber, Depew, and Smith 1988\).)Tj
/Fc 11 Tf -7.81 -26.4 Td(\267 )Tj
/F4 11 Tf 7.81 -13.2 Td(Holland's)Tj
/F6 11 Tf ( Adaptation in Natural and Artificial Systems)Tj
/F4 11 Tf (, in which GAs were defined, was one of the)Tj
-0 -13.2 Td(first attempts to set down a general framework for adaptation in nature and in computers. Holland's)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(work has had considerable influence on the thinking of scientists in many fields, and it set the stage)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(for most of the subsequent work on GA theory. However, Holland's theory is not a complete)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(description of GA behavior. Recently a number of other approaches, such as exact mathematical)Tj
-0 -13.2 Td(models, statistical-mechanics-based models, and results from population genetics, have gained)Tj
-0 -13.2 Td(considerable attention. GA theory is not just academic; theoretical advances must be made so that we)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(can know how best to use GAs and how to characterize the types of problems for which they are)Tj
/F8 11 Tf 198.326 -24.782 Td(135)Tj
