/F4 11 Tf 36 687 Td 0.000 Tc(suited. I believe that theoretical advances will also filter back to the evolutionary biology community.)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Though it hasn't happened yet, I think there is a very good chance that proving things about these)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(simple models will lead to new ways to think mathematically about natural evolution.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(Evolutionary computation is far from being an established science with a body of knowledge that has)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(been collected for centuries. It has been around for little more than 30 years, and only in the last)Tj
-0 -13.2 Td(decade have a reasonably large number of people been working on it. Almost all the projects)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(discussed in this book still can be considered "work in progress." The projects described here were)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(chosen because I find the work, or at least its general direction, worth pursuing. In each of the case)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(studies I have tried to point out open questions and to give some ideas about what should be done)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(next. My strong hope is that readers of this book will become excited or inspired enough to take some)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(of this research further, or even to take genetic algorithms in new directions. Here is a brief list of)Tj
-0 -13.2 Td(some of the directions I think are the most important and promising.)Tj
/F9 15.8 Tf -36 -57.64 Td(Incorporating Ecological Interactions)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -40.568 Td(In most GA applications the candidate solutions in the population are assigned fitnesses independent of one)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(another and interact only by competing for selection slots via their fitnesses. However, some of the more)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(interesting and successful applications have used more complicated "ecological" interactions among)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(population members. Hillis's host-parasite)Tj
( coevolution was a prime example; so was Axelrod's experiment in)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(which the evolving strategies for the Prisoner's Dilemma played against one another and developed a)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(cooperative symbiosis. These methods \(along with other examples in the GA literature\) are not understood)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(very well; much more work is needed, for example, on making host-parasite coevolution a more generally)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(applicable method and understanding how it works. In addition, other types of ecological interactions, such as)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(individual competition for resources or symbiotic cooperation in collective problem solving, can be utilized in)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(GAs.)Tj
/F9 15.8 Tf 0 -57.64 Td(Incorporating New Ideas from Genetics)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -27.368 Td(Haploid crossover and mutation are only the barest bones of real-world genetic systems. I have discussed)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(some extensions, including diploidy, inversion, gene doubling, and deletion. Other GA researchers have)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(looked at genetics-inspired mechanisms such as dominance, translocation, sexual differentiation \(Goldberg)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(1989a, chapter 5\), and introns \(Levenick 1991\). These all are likely to have important roles in nature, and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(mechanisms inspired by them could potentially be put to excellent use in problem solving with GAs. As yet,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the exploration of such mechanisms has only barely scratched the surface of their potential. Perhaps even)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(more potentially significant is genetic regulation. In recent years a huge amount has been learned in the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(genetics community about how genes regulate one another\227how they turn one another on and off in)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(complicated ways so that only the appropriate genes get expressed in a given situation. It is these regulatory)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(networks that make the genome a complex but extremely adaptive system. Capturing this kind of genetic)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(adaptivity will be increasingly important as GAs are used in more complicated, changing environments.)Tj
/F8 11 Tf 132.235 625.416 Td(Chapter 6: Conclusions and Future Directions)Tj
102.091 -720 Td(136)Tj
