/F9 15.8 Tf 0 682.16 Td 0.000 Tc(Incorporating Development and Learning)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -27.368 Td(Whereas in typical GA applications evolution works directly on a population of candidate solutions, in nature)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(there is a separation between genotypes \(encodings\) and phenotypes \(candidate solutions\). There are very)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(good reasons for such a separation. One is that, as organisms become more complex, it seems to be more)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(efficient and tractable for the operators of evolution to work on a simpler encoding that develops into the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(complex organism. Another is that environments are often too unpredictable for appropriate behavior to be)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(directly encoded into a genotype that does not change during an individual's life. In nature, the processes of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(development and learning help "tune" the behavioral parameters defined by the individual's genome so that)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the individual's behavior will become adapted for its particular environment. These reasons for separating)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(genotype from phenotype and for incorporating development and learning have been seen in several of our)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(case studies. Kitano pointed out that a grammatical encoding followed by a development phase allows)Tj
( for the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(evolution of more complex neural networks than is possible using a direct encoding. Incorporating)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(development in this way has been taken further by Gruau \(1992\) and Belew \(1993\), but much more work)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(needs to be done if we are to use GAs to evolve large, complex sytems \(such as computational "brains"\). The)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(same can be said for incorporating learning into evolutionary computation\227we have seen how this can have)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(many advantages, even if what is learned is not directly transmitted to offspring\227but the simulations we have)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(seen are only early steps in understanding how to best take advantage of interactions between evolution and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(learning.)Tj
/F9 15.8 Tf 0 -57.64 Td(Adapting Encodings and Using Encodings That Permit)Tj
0 -19.008 Td(Hierarchy and Open-Endedness)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -27.368 Td(Evolution in nature not only changes the fitnesses of organisms, it also has mechanisms for changing the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(genetic encoding. I have discussed some reordering operators that occur in nature \(e.g., inversion and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(translocation\). In addition, genotypes have increased in size over evolutionary time. In chapter 5 I gave many)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(reasons why the ability to adapt their own encodings is important for GAs. Several methods have been)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(explored in the GA literature. In my opinion, if we want GAs eventually to be able to evolve complex)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(structures, the most important factors will be open-endedness \(the ability for evolution to increase the size)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(and complexity of individuals to an arbitrary degree\), encapsulation \(the ability to protect a useful part of an)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(encoding from genetic disruption and to make sure it acts as a single whole\), and hierarchical regulation \(the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(ability to have different parts of the genome regulate other parts, and likewise the ability to have different)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(parts of the phenotype regulate other parts\). Some explorations of open-endedness and encapsulation in)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(genetic programming were discussed; to me these types of explorations seem to be on the right track, though)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(the specific type of encoding used in GP may not turn out to be the most effective one for evolving complex)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(structures.)Tj
/F9 15.8 Tf 0 -57.64 Td(Adapting Parameters)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -27.368 Td(Natural evolution adapts its own parameters. Crossover and mutation rates are encoded \(presumably in some)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(rather complicated way\) in the genomes of organisms, along with places where these operators are more likely)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(to be applied \(e.g., crossover hot spots\). Likewise, population sizes in nature are not constant but are)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(controlled by complicated ecological interactions. As was described in chapter 5, we would like to find similar)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(ways of adapting the parameters for GAs as part of the evolutionary process. There have been several)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(explorations of this already, but much more work needs to be done to develop more general and sophisticated)Tj
/F8 11 Tf 132.235 689.832 Td(Chapter 6: Conclusions and Future Directions)Tj
102.091 -720 Td(137)Tj
