/F4 11 Tf 0 687 Td 0.000 Tc(techniques. As was also mentioned in chapter 5, one of the major difficulties is having the time scale of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(adaptation for the parameters appropriately match the time scale of adaptation of the individuals in the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(population. As far as I know, no theoretical work has been done on this in the GA literature. The development)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(of such a theory is a very important future direction.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(The directions listed above are important both for making GAs more sophisticated problem solvers and for)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(using them to understand evolutionary systems in nature. The following are some important directions for GA)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(theory:)Tj
/F9 15.8 Tf 0 -57.64 Td(Connections with the Mathematical Genetics Literature)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -27.368 Td(The GA theory community has not paid enough attention to what has already been done in the related field of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(mathematical genetics, though this is changing to some degree \(see, e.g., Booker 1993 and Altenberg 1995\).)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(There is much more to be learned there that is of potential interest to GA theory.)Tj
/F9 15.8 Tf 0 -57.64 Td(Extension of Statistical Mechanics Approaches)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -27.368 Td(As I said in chapter 4, I think approaches similar to that taken by Prugel-Bennett and Shapiro are promising)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(for better understanding the behavior of GAs. That is, rather than construct exact mathematical models that in)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(effect take into account every individual in a population, it is more useful to understand how macroscopic)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(population structures change as a result of evolution. Ultimately we would like to have a general theory of the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(evolution of such macroscopic structures that will predict the effects of changes in parameters and other)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(details of the GA. There is much more to be mined from the field of statistical mechanics in formulating such)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(theories.)Tj
/F9 15.8 Tf 0 -57.64 Td(Identifying and Overcoming Impediments to the Success of GAs)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -27.368 Td(In the case studies and in the theoretical discussion we came across many potential impediments to the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(success of GAs, including deception, hitchhiking, symmetry breaking, overfitting, and inadequate sampling.)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(GA researchers do not yet have anywhere near a complete understanding of the precise effects of these and)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(other impediments on the performance of GAs, or of the precise conditions under which they come about, or)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(of how to overcome them if that is possible.)Tj
/F9 15.8 Tf 0 -57.64 Td(Understanding the Role of Schemas in GAs)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -27.368 Td(As readers of chapter 4 have no doubt gleaned, there is still a controversy in the GA community over the)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(proper role of "schemas" in understanding GAs. This role must be pinned down and agreed on.)Tj
/F8 11 Tf 132.235 637.032 Td(Chapter 6: Conclusions and Future Directions)Tj
