/F9 15.8 Tf 0 682.16 Td 0.000 Tc(Understanding the Role of Crossover)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -27.368 Td(Crossover is the primary operator distinguishing GAs from other stochastic search methods, but its role in)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(GAs needs to be better understood.)Tj
( Under what conditions does it indeed recombine building blocks to form)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(high-fitness solutions, and under what conditions is it instead serving only as a "macro-mutation" operator,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(simply making larger jumps in the search space than a simple mutation operator can make? What is its role)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(during various stages of the search? How can we quantify its ability to construct good solutions? Much)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(theoretical work on GAs is aimed at answering these questions \(e.g., the experiments on Royal Road functions)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(described in chapter 4\), but precise answers are still lacking.)Tj
/F9 15.8 Tf 0 -57.64 Td(Theory of GAs With Endogenous Fitness)Tj
/F4 11 Tf 0 -27.368 Td(In many of the scientific models we have looked at, "fitness" is not externally imposed but instead arises)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(endogenously; it is reflected, for example, by the longevity and the fertility of an individual. Up to now,)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(almost all work in GA theory has assumed exogenous rather than endogenous fitness functions. Holland)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(\(1994\) has recently done some theoretical work on GA behavior with endogenous fitness in the context of)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(Echo, using notions such a "flow-matrix" for describing the transmission of useful genetic building blocks)Tj
0 -13.2 Td(from generation to generation. This is only a first step in theoretically analyzing such systems.)Tj
0 -26.4 Td(There are many open questions, and there is much important work to be done. Readers, onward!)Tj
/F8 11 Tf 132.235 321.816 Td(Chapter 6: Conclusions and Future Directions)Tj
102.091 -720 Td(139)Tj
ET
Q
